To add Sign in with GitHub, you redirect the user to` https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize` , receive a temporary` code` at your callback URL, and exchange that code for an access token by POSTing to` https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token` from your server.


You have the OAuth app settings open in one tab and your route handler in another, and most tutorials hand the whole job to an SDK. This guide builds the server flow in Node using` fetch` and Express, with no OAuth library. For the underlying model, see[All about OAuth 2.0](https://blog.openreplay.com/all-about-oauth-2-0/) . It covers state and PKCE, the null-email problem, scopes, and the redirect URI and token refresh changes GitHub released on August 14, 2026.


## Key Takeaways


- GitHub’s authorization code expires 10 minutes after it is issued, and the code exchange must run on your server because it requires the client secret.
- Generate` state` on the server, store it in an httpOnly cookie, and abort the callback if the returned value differs. This blocks login CSRF.
- ` GET https://api.github.com/user` returns` email: null` when the user has no public email, so request` user:email` and use the primary, verified entry from` GET /user/emails` .
- Since GitHub’s August 14, 2026 update, an OAuth app can register up to 10 redirect URIs, and new apps issue 8-hour access tokens with 6-month refresh tokens by default.


## Register the GitHub OAuth App


Create an OAuth app under Settings → Developer settings → OAuth Apps, then copy the client ID and generate a client secret.[GitHub’s registration guide](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/creating-an-oauth-app) lists every field.


[Since the August 14, 2026 release](https://github.blog/changelog/2026-08-14-multiple-redirect-uris-and-token-refresh-for-oauth-apps/) , one OAuth app can hold up to 10 redirect URIs, which you add with the **Add callback URL** button. You can cover production, preview deployments and local development with a single app. For local work, register` http://127.0.0.1:3000/auth/github/callback` .` localhost` works, but[GitHub’s loopback guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps#loopback-redirect-urls) points out that the OAuth RFC recommends the` 127.0.0.1` literal.


Wildcard matching is now a setting on each redirect URI. If your app had just one callback URL before August 3, 2026, GitHub has turned wildcard matching on for it to keep the old redirect behavior. Turn it off unless you control every subdomain and path beneath that URL.


## Redirect to GitHub’s Authorize Endpoint


The sign-in button points to a server route. That route generates` state` and a PKCE verifier, stores both in short-lived cookies, and redirects to` GET https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize` .


```text
import   crypto   from   '  node:crypto  '  ;
import   express   from   '  express  '  ;
import   cookieParser   from   '  cookie-parser  '  ;


const   app   =   express  ();
app  .  use  (  cookieParser  ());


const   {   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID  ,   GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET  ,   GITHUB_REDIRECT_URI   }   =   process  .  env  ;
// GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET is read only here, on the server. Never ship it to the browser.
const   isProd   =   process  .  env  .  NODE_ENV   ===   '  production  '  ;


const   tempCookie   =   {
httpOnly  :   true  ,
secure  :   isProd  ,
sameSite  :   '  lax  '  ,
maxAge  :   10   *   60   *   1000  ,   // matches the 10-minute code lifetime
path  :   '  /auth/github  '  ,
};


app  .  get  (  '  /auth/github  '  , (  req  ,   res  )   =>   {
const   state   =   crypto  .  randomBytes  (  32  ).  toString  (  '  base64url  '  );
const   verifier   =   crypto  .  randomBytes  (  32  ).  toString  (  '  base64url  '  );   // 43 chars
const   challenge   =   crypto  .  createHash  (  '  sha256  '  ).  update  (  verifier  ).  digest  (  '  base64url  '  );


res  .  cookie  (  '  gh_state  '  ,   state  ,   tempCookie  );
res  .  cookie  (  '  gh_verifier  '  ,   verifier  ,   tempCookie  );


const   params   =   new   URLSearchParams  ({
client_id  :   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID  ,
redirect_uri  :   GITHUB_REDIRECT_URI  ,   // one value per environment
scope  :   '  read:user user:email  '  ,
state  ,
code_challenge  :   challenge  ,
code_challenge_method  :   '  S256  '  ,
});
res  .  redirect  (  `  https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize?  ${  params  }`  );
});
```


[GitHub’s authorize parameters](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps#1-request-a-users-github-identity) support one PKCE method,` S256` , and reject` plain` . The challenge has to be a 43-character SHA-256 value, which is exactly what base64url-encoding a SHA-256 digest gives you. If users often have more than one GitHub account, add` prompt=select_account` so GitHub always shows the account picker.


## Validate state to Stop Login CSRF


The` state` parameter is a random value that no one can guess. Your server creates it and stores it in the session or an httpOnly cookie. When GitHub redirects back, compare it with the` state` on the callback. If the two differ, stop the sign-in and do not exchange the code.


Without that check, your app is open to login CSRF, an attack that signs the victim in to the attacker’s account:


1. The attacker starts your GitHub sign-in flow with their own GitHub account.
2. They approve access but stop before the callback runs, keeping their valid` code` .
3. They get the victim’s browser to load` https://yourapp.com/auth/github/callback?code=ATTACKER_CODE` , for example through a hidden image or an auto-submitting link.
4. Your server exchanges the code and signs the victim in as the attacker. Worse, if the victim was already logged in, it links the attacker’s GitHub identity to the victim’s account.
5. Anything the victim then saves lands in an account the attacker controls. A linked identity also lets the attacker sign in as the victim later.


With` state` , step 4 fails. The victim’s browser either has no` gh_state` cookie or holds one that doesn’t match the attacker’s value, so the server rejects the request. PKCE adds a second binding: the code only works together with the` code_verifier` stored in the browser that started the flow.[RFC 6749](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6749) defines` state` for this purpose, and the[OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700) recommends PKCE for every client. The[state and PKCE guidance in Securing OAuth best practices](https://blog.openreplay.com/best-practices-securing-oauth-web/) goes deeper.


```text
function   statesMatch  (  received  ,   stored  ) {
if (  typeof   received   !==   '  string  '   ||   typeof   stored   !==   '  string  '  )   return   false  ;
const   a   =   Buffer  .  from  (  received  );
const   b   =   Buffer  .  from  (  stored  );
return   a  .  length   ===   b  .  length   &&   crypto  .  timingSafeEqual  (  a  ,   b  );
}
```


The[cookie attributes](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Set-Cookie) matter as well.` HttpOnly` keeps scripts away from the value, and` Secure` restricts it to HTTPS.` SameSite=Lax` lets the cookie survive GitHub’s top-level redirect back to you. A single` gh_state` cookie means a second sign-in tab overwrites the first, so show a clear retry message rather than a blank error.


## Handle the Callback and Exchange the Code


The callback checks` state` , clears the temporary cookies, and POSTs the code to` https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token` with` Accept: application/json` . Without that header,[GitHub’s token endpoint](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps#2-users-are-redirected-back-to-your-site-by-github) replies with a form-encoded body. The code expires 10 minutes after it is issued, so exchange it right away.


```text
app  .  get  (  '  /auth/github/callback  '  ,   async   (  req  ,   res  )   =>   {
const   {   code  ,   state   }   =   req  .  query  ;
const   { gh_state:   savedState  , gh_verifier:   verifier   }   =   req  .  cookies  ;


res  .  clearCookie  (  '  gh_state  '  , {   path  :   '  /auth/github  '   });
res  .  clearCookie  (  '  gh_verifier  '  , {   path  :   '  /auth/github  '   });


if (  typeof   code   !==   '  string  '   ||   !  verifier   ||   !  statesMatch  (  state  ,   savedState  )) {
return   res  .  status  (  400  ).  send  (  '  Sign-in failed. Please try again.  '  );
}


const   tokenRes   =   await   fetch  (  '  https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token  '  , {
method  :   '  POST  '  ,
headers  :   {
Accept  :   '  application/json  '  ,
'  Content-Type  '  :   '  application/x-www-form-urlencoded  '  ,
},
body  :   new   URLSearchParams  ({
client_id  :   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID  ,
client_secret  :   GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET  ,
code  ,
redirect_uri  :   GITHUB_REDIRECT_URI  ,   // must match the authorize request
code_verifier  :   verifier  ,
}),
});
const   token   =   await   tokenRes  .  json  ();
if (  token  .  error   ||   !  token  .  access_token  ) {
}


const   profile   =   await   getGitHubProfile  (  token  .  access_token  );
const   user   =   await   upsertUserByGitHubId  (  profile  );   // key on profile.id
await   saveGitHubTokens  (  user  .  id  ,   token  );             // server-side only
await   createSession  (  res  ,   user  .  id  );                  // your own session cookie
res  .  redirect  (  '  /  '  );
});
```


Keep this exchange on the server, because it needs the client secret and that secret must never reach the browser. Once the exchange succeeds, issue your own session cookie and keep the GitHub token server-side.[Cookies vs localStorage for JWT auth](https://blog.openreplay.com/cookies-vs-localstorage-jwt-auth/) covers the storage trade-offs. The final` res.redirect('/')` removes` code` and` state` from the address bar, so a Back press can’t replay a spent code.


Session replays of failed GitHub sign-ins show the full sequence: click, GitHub, callback, error page. That sequence separates state mismatches from expired codes. Configure any replay, analytics or logging tool to mask the` code` and` state` query parameters.


## Fetch the User and a Verified Email


Call` GET https://api.github.com/user` with the token to identify the user.[The authenticated-user endpoint](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/users/users) returns` email: null` when the user hasn’t set a public email. OAuth tokens also need` read:user` to get the private user response.


```text
const   ghHeaders   =   (  accessToken  )   =>   ({
Authorization  :   `  Bearer   ${  accessToken  }`  ,
Accept  :   '  application/vnd.github+json  '  ,
'  User-Agent  '  :   '  your-app-name  '  ,
});


async   function   getGitHubProfile  (  accessToken  ) {
const   userRes   =   await   fetch  (  '  https://api.github.com/user  '  , {   headers  :   ghHeaders  (  accessToken  )   });
if (  !  userRes  .  ok  )   throw   new   Error  (  `  GET /user failed:   ${  userRes  .  status  }`  );
const   user   =   await   userRes  .  json  ();


let   email   =   user  .  email  ;
if (  !  email  ) {
const   emailsRes   =   await   fetch  (  '  https://api.github.com/user/emails  '  , {   headers  :   ghHeaders  (  accessToken  )   });
if (  emailsRes  .  ok  ) {
const   emails   =   await   emailsRes  .  json  ();
email   =   emails  .  find  ((  e  )   =>   e  .  primary   &&   e  .  verified  )?.  email   ??   null  ;
}
}
return   {   id  :   user  .  id  ,   login  :   user  .  login  ,   name  :   user  .  name  ,   email   };
}
```


With the` user:email` scope,[GET /user/emails](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/users/emails) returns every address along with` primary` and` verified` flags. Pick the entry where both are true, and never link accounts on an unverified address. Key users on the numeric` id` , because logins and emails can change.[GitHub’s REST API refuses any request that has no User-Agent header](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/getting-started-with-the-rest-api#user-agent) , so set one yourself.[GitHub also advises checking who the user is each time you get a new token](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps#3-use-the-access-token-to-access-the-api) , since the user may have switched accounts on the authorize page.


## Request the Least Scope You Need


Sign in with GitHub needs only two OAuth scopes:` read:user` and` user:email` .[GitHub’s scope reference](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/scopes-for-oauth-apps) defines each one, and users see every scope you ask for on GitHub’s consent screen.


You need Scope


Profile: id, login, name, avatar` read:user`


Email addresses` user:email`


Repository contents` repo` (full access, including private repos)


Requesting` repo` for sign-in asks users to hand over read/write access to their private code, and many will stop at that point. If you need repository access for a later feature, run a separate flow at that point.[GitHub allows at most ten tokens for each user, app and scope combination, and no more than ten new tokens per hour](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps#creating-multiple-tokens-for-oauth-apps) . If a callback loop hits that hourly limit, GitHub stops and shows the user a re-authorization prompt.


## Refresh Expiring Tokens


To refresh a GitHub token, POST` grant_type=refresh_token` to the token endpoint. Using a refresh token retires it along with the old access token, so save the new pair before making another call. With expiring tokens on,[access tokens expire after eight hours, and an unused refresh token expires after six months](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps#expiring-access-tokens) . Since the August 14, 2026 change, new apps get this behavior by default. Existing apps can opt in for a single sign-in by adding the` offline_access` scope, which doesn’t add a consent prompt, or they can enable it in the app settings.


```text
async   function   refreshGitHubToken  (  refreshToken  ) {
// No refresh token means a non-expiring token: nothing to refresh.
if (  !  refreshToken  )   return   null  ;


const   res   =   await   fetch  (  '  https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token  '  , {
method  :   '  POST  '  ,
headers  :   {
Accept  :   '  application/json  '  ,
'  Content-Type  '  :   '  application/x-www-form-urlencoded  '  ,
},
body  :   new   URLSearchParams  ({
client_id  :   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID  ,
client_secret  :   GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET  ,
grant_type  :   '  refresh_token  '  ,
refresh_token  :   refreshToken  ,
}),
});
const   data   =   await   res  .  json  ();
if (  data  .  error   ===   '  bad_refresh_token  '  )   return   {   reauth  :   true   };
if (  data  .  error  )   throw   new   Error  (  data  .  error  );


return   {
accessToken  :   data  .  access_token  ,
refreshToken  :   data  .  refresh_token  ,
expiresAt  :   Date  .  now  ()   +   data  .  expires_in   *   1000  ,
};
}
```


You can’t change scopes during a refresh.` bad_refresh_token` means the user has to sign in again. Don’t assume a` refresh_token` comes back at all: some GitHub Enterprise Server versions ignore` offline_access` and issue a non-expiring token.


## Should You Use an OAuth App or a GitHub App?


For Sign in with GitHub alone, an OAuth app requesting` read:user user:email` is enough, even though GitHub recommends GitHub Apps over OAuth apps in general.[GitHub’s comparison page](https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/differences-between-github-apps-and-oauth-apps) covers the full differences.


OAuth app GitHub App


Permission model Broad scopes Fine-grained permissions


Repository selection All repos the scope covers User picks repositories


Token lifetime 8-hour expiring by default for new apps; long-lived for older apps unless enabled Short-lived


GitHub’s recommendation Supported Preferred


Choose a GitHub App when your product acts on repositories. For identity, the OAuth app flow above is enough.


## Security Checklist


- Send` state` and PKCE` S256` on every request, and verify both on the callback.
- Send the same` redirect_uri` in the authorize request and the token request.
- Disable wildcard matching you don’t need.
- Keep the client secret and GitHub tokens on the server. Give the browser only your own session cookie.
- Redirect away from the callback after the exchange.
- Handle a token response that has no` refresh_token` .


Sign in with GitHub comes down to three requests: authorize, exchange, fetch the user. The security lives in the details around them: a server-held` state` , a PKCE verifier, a secret that never leaves your backend, and an email you’ve confirmed is verified. Next, open your OAuth app settings, add your preview and` 127.0.0.1` callback URLs, turn off wildcard matching, and ship the three routes above.


## FAQs


Why does GitHub's token endpoint return bad_verification_code?


GitHub returns bad_verification_code when the code you send is incorrect, expired, or does not match the one issued in the authorization request. The usual causes are a code older than 10 minutes, or a code that was already exchanged, for example after a Back press or a second request to the callback. You cannot recover that code. Restart the sign-in flow to get a new one.


What causes redirect_uri_mismatch in GitHub OAuth?


GitHub returns redirect_uri_mismatch when the redirect_uri you send does not match a callback URL registered on the OAuth app. Send a registered URL exactly, or leave the parameter out and GitHub uses the app's default. Send the same value on the authorize request and the token request. GitHub treats 127.0.0.1 and localhost as different hosts, so register the one your local server uses.


Does signing out of my app revoke the user's GitHub token?


No. Ending your own session leaves the GitHub token and the authorization valid. To revoke one token, call DELETE /applications/{client_id}/token. To remove every token and the app's authorization for that user, call DELETE /applications/{client_id}/grant. Both endpoints use Basic authentication with the client_id and client_secret, plus the access_token in the request body. Users can also revoke access in their GitHub account settings.


Can a non-expiring GitHub OAuth token still stop working?


Yes. GitHub automatically revokes an OAuth token that goes unused for one year. A token also stops working when the user revokes your app's authorization, or when the token is pushed to a public repository or gist. If one user, app and scope combination collects more than ten tokens, GitHub revokes one of the older ones. When a stored token stops working, send the user through sign-in again.


